Okay.  I've been playing hack on Unix systems since version 1.03.  I
remember back when engraving "Elbereth" was considered a form of
cheating.

First, comments on 3.1:  I'm glad that the Dev Team is slowly but
surely closing up points of abuse (polypiling, digging through shops,
etc.).  I'm disturbed, however, by the number of "cheats" that a lot
of players seem to want or demand.  

I actually don't have too much of a problem with working from save
games: hey, if that's how you want to play, be my guest.  What bothers
me is people whining about how something that makes things hideously
easy isn't *built* *in* to the game.  Hey, play in wizard mode if you
want that (you all know what I'm talking about: "I think there should
be a sword called MonsterSlayer that you get from #dipping into sinks,
which kills monsters instantly 50% of the time..."  The Stormbringer
"controversy" is a good example).  In my opinion, everything that
makes the game easier should have an equal and opposite negative
reaction.  Such is life.

Anyway, a few quick suggestions, if anyone is still reading :-).
First, there is no rational reason that pets should be able to steal
from shops, unless the shopkeeper has an IQ of around room
temperature.  There should be only three ways to get things from
shops:  pay the shopkeeper, teleport out and deal with the Kops, or
kill the shopkeeper with good honest violence.  As it is, a lawful
character can use his pet to steal for him without pissing off his
God: this is wrong.

I'll post more later,

